                "name": "Introduction of ER Model",
                "lastUpdated": "2025-11-17T00:00:00.000Z",
                "content": [
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "The Entity-Relationship Model (ER Model) is a conceptual model for designing a database. This model represents the logical structure of a database, including entities, their attributes and relationships between them."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "list",
                        "title": "Basic Components",
                        "items": [
                            "Entity: An object stored as data such as Student, Course or Company.",
                            "Attribute: Properties that describe an entity such as StudentID, CourseName, or EmployeeEmail.",
                            "Relationship: A connection between entities such as 'a Student enrolls in a Course'."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Components of ER Diagram"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "The graphical representation of an ER model is called an Entity-Relationship Diagram (ERD)."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "ER Model in Database Design Process"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "list",
                        "items": [
                            "Gather the requirements (functional and data) by asking questions to the database users.",
                            "Create a conceptual design using the ER model.",
                            "Convert ERD to a logical model.",
                            "Later move toward physical database design (indexing, storage) and external design (views)."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Why Use ER Diagrams in DBMS?"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "list",
                        "items": [
                            "ER diagrams easily convert into relational tables.",
                            "They visually represent real-world objects and relationships.",
                            "Users don't need technical DBMS knowledge to understand ERDs.",
                            "They simplify complex data structure visualization."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Symbols Used in ER Model"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "ER models use standardized symbols to represent entities, attributes, and relationships."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "What is an Entity?"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "An Entity represents a real-world object, concept, or thing about which data is stored in a database. In relational databases, entities map to tables."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "list",
                        "title": "Examples of Entities",
                        "items": [
                            "Person, Car, Employee",
                            "Course, Event, Reservation",
                            "Product, Document, Device"
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "What is an Entity Set?"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "An entity set is the collection of all entities of a particular entity type."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Types of Entity"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "numbered_list",
                        "items": [
                            "Strong Entity – Has a primary key and does not rely on another entity for identification.",
                            "Weak Entity – Cannot be uniquely identified without a strong entity; represented by double rectangles."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Attributes in ER Model"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "Attributes define properties of an entity. Example: For Student → Roll_No, Name, DOB, Age, Phone_No."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Types of Attributes"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "numbered_list",
                        "items": [
                            "Key Attribute – Uniquely identifies each entity (e.g., Roll_No).",
                            "Composite Attribute – Composed of multiple attributes (e.g., Address → City, State, Country).",
                            "Multivalued Attribute – Has multiple values (e.g., Phone_No).",
                            "Derived Attribute – Computed from another attribute (e.g., Age derived from DOB)."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Relationship Type and Relationship Set"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "paragraph",
                        "text": "A relationship type describes associations between entities. A relationship set is a collection of similar relationships."
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Degree of Relationship"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "numbered_list",
                        "items": [
                            "Unary/Recursive Relationship – One entity participates (e.g., Person married to Person).",
                            "Binary Relationship – Two entity sets participate (e.g., Student–Course).",
                            "Ternary Relationship – Three entity sets participate.",
                            "N-ary Relationship – More than three entity sets participate."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Cardinality in ER Model"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "numbered_list",
                        "items": [
                            "One-to-One – A person has one passport.",
                            "One-to-Many – One department has many doctors.",
                            "Many-to-One – Many surgeries done by one surgeon.",
                            "Many-to-Many – Employees work on multiple projects."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "Participation Constraints"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "numbered_list",
                        "items": [
                            "Total Participation – Every entity must participate (double line).",
                            "Partial Participation – Participation is optional."
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "heading",
                        "text": "How to Draw an ER Diagram"
                    },
                    {
                        "type": "numbered_list",
                        "items": [
                            "Identify Entities.",
                            "Identify Relationships.",
                            "Add Attributes.",
                            "Define Primary Keys.",
                            "Remove Redundancy.",
                            "Review for clarity."
                        ]
                    }
                ]
            }
            ,

            { "name": "ER Model" },
